## Bug #2 fixed: day arithmetic across the year-100 boundary

### Root cause
`objToLocalTS()` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` builds a timestamp with `Date.UTC(year, ...)`. JS maps years 0–99 to 1900–1999, so for `year: 99, month: 12, day: 92` (0099-12-31 + 61 days, where `adjustTime()` in datetime.js passes an overflowing day) `Date.UTC` normalized the overflow using **year 2000 — a leap year** → `2000-03-01`. The old code then only shifted the year back:

```js
d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);   // -> 0100-03-01  (WRONG)
```

This discards the month/day re-normalization, so the extra leap day of 2000 was kept even though 0100 is not a leap year (divisible by 100, not 400). `isLeapYear`/`daysInMonth`/`daysInYear` themselves are correct (`isLeapYear(100) === false`, `daysInMonth(100,2) === 28`); the bug was purely in the year-0..99 timestamp construction.

### Change (only file touched: `/luxon/src/impl/util.js`, lines 166–172)
```diff
@@ -166,7 +166,10 @@ export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
   // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
   if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
     d = new Date(d);
-    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
+    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
+    // so if obj.year is in 99, but obj.day makes it roll over into year 100,
+    // the calculations done by Date.UTC are using year 2000 - which is a leap year - and not year 100
+    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
   }
   return +d;
 }
```
